// pf_collapse.vfl - PRIM wrangle, immediately after an inset.
//
// An inset polygon whose signed area FLIPPED or vanished has folded through
// itself: the setback was deeper than the lot, or the courtyard tract deeper
// than half the block. §2.2 says advisory, never a wall - so this records the
// fact on the prim and lets B2 degrade to one solid volume, which is what a
// building on a lot too small for its courtyard actually is.
//
// It also refreshes `_area0` in place, because the courtyard pass insets this
// same polygon again and needs the CURRENT winding, not the lot's.
int pts[] = primpoints(0, @primnum);
float a = 0.0;
for (int k = 0; k < len(pts); k++) {
    vector p = point(0, "P", pts[k]);
    vector r = point(0, "P", pts[(k + 1) % len(pts)]);
    a += p.x * r.z - r.x * p.z;
}
a *= 0.5;
float was = f@_area0;

// THREE TESTS, and the middle one is the one that was missing.  A sign flip
// catches a single inversion; it does NOT catch a DOUBLE inversion, which
// restores the sign and looks healthy.  Measured by an audit: with every
// setback at 40 m, footprint areas came out [-700, 640, 756, 3402] and only
// the first was flagged - a 17 x 26 m lot (442 m2) produced a 3 402 m2
// "footprint" and was massed far outside the lot it belongs to.
//
// An inward offset of a simple polygon can only REDUCE its area.  So area
// that GREW is proof of a fold, whatever the sign did, and it costs nothing.
//
// ⚠️ Strictly grew.  `>=` is wrong and was measured wrong: `setback(0)` is
// the identity op §12.6 B1 names, the Viennese block and the row house both
// use it on every edge, and equality flagged both of them as collapsed - two
// perimeter blocks silently degraded to one solid mass while every other
// check stayed green.
i@pf_warn_footprint_collapsed =
    (a * was <= 0.0 || abs(a) > abs(was) * (1.0 + 1e-6) + 1e-6
     || abs(a) < 1e-4) ? 1 : 0;
f@_area0 = a;
